🤖 Agent: That’s a meaningful project — and there are several paths you can take to learn more about your great‑aunt’s wartime/refugee experience. Below I’ve organized practical steps, key archives and databases to contact, and tips for searching so you can make steady progress.

If you want, tell me what you already know (full name, maiden name, birth date/place, approximate year she came to the U.S., port of arrival, any U.S. city she settled in) and I can help you draft search queries, emails, or FOIA/genealogy requests.

1) Gather every fact you already have (the essentials)
- Full name (including maiden and married names if applicable)  
- Exact or approximate birth date and birthplace (town/village)  
- Any known alternate spellings, nicknames or transliterations  
- Year (or range) of immigration/arrival to the U.S. and port (if known)  
- Town(s) in the U.S. where she lived (first address helps)  
- Any relatives’ names who may appear on immigration/ship lists  
- Copies or photos of any documents you already have (naturalization certificate, ship ticket, passport, visa, social security application, obituary, etc.)

2) Core U.S. records and where to search/request them
- Passenger arrival lists / ship manifests — NARA (National Archives) has passenger lists for arrivals at U.S. ports (Ellis Island/New York, Boston, Baltimore, etc.). Also searchable on commercial sites (Ancestry, FamilySearch) and the Statue of Liberty/Ellis Island site.  
- Naturalization records — often contain arrival info, birthplaces, and sometimes exact origin towns. NARA and local U.S. federal courts or county clerk offices hold these; USCIS Genealogy Program can provide older naturalization files.  
- Census records (1940, 1950 released) — show household members, year of immigration and naturalization status. Available on NARA, Ancestry, FamilySearch.  
- Social Security Applications (SS-5) — often include birth date/place and parents’ names; you can request via SSA FOIA for deceased persons.  
- City directories, newspapers, obituaries — local libraries, historical societies, or NewspaperArchive/GenealogyBank can help.

3) Key international archives and refugee‑specific resources
- Arolsen Archives (formerly International Tracing Service / ITS) — the central archive for displaced persons (DP) and refugee records from WWII and the immediate postwar period. They have digital search tools and can respond to individual queries. Essential for DP camp, transport, and registration records.  
- United States Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — has collections and databases (and often links to ITS/Arolsen). Their research staff can advise.  
- International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) Tracing Service — handled tracing and family reunification; useful for wartime refugee records.  
- UNRRA / IRO records (United Nations Relief/International Refugee Organization) — records about displaced persons and resettlement; some records are in national archives or Arolsen.  
- German archives: Bundesarchiv, regional Landesarchiv or the local Standesamt (civil registry) for birth records or pre‑war residence registers (Meldebuch). Church/parish records (Kirchenbücher) are very valuable if you have a town name.

4) Context & legal history that helps your search
- Many European refugees after WWII were processed through DP camps and resettled under UN or U.S. resettlement programs (Displaced Persons Act 1948, IRO). Knowing approximate year(s) helps focus whether to search DP records (late 1940s) vs. pre-war emigration lists.  
- If she left Germany during the Nazi era (1933–45), there may be emigration, refugee or visa files (some are held in national archives, some digitized).

5) Practical search workflow (step‑by‑step)
A. Start with what’s easiest: US records you can access online (Ancestry, FamilySearch, NARA catalog). Search ship manifests, census, naturalization indexes using all name variants.  
B. If you find an arrival record, note ship name, date, port — this unlocks many other searches.  
C. Request naturalization file or an SS‑5 copy if you have a death or SSN record.  
D. If U.S. searches stall, contact Arolsen Archives/USHMM with your great‑aunt’s full name, birth date/place and any known DP camp or transit details. They accept inquiries and can often locate DP records.  
E. For town‑level German records, use regional archive portals or familysearch.org’s German parish catalog (if you have the town name).  
F. If records are sparse, consider DNA + DNA communities (GEDmatch, Ancestry DNA matches) to find relatives who might have more documentation.

6) How to contact archives & what to include
- Use a concise, polite email. Include: full name(s), date/place of birth, approximate dates of migration, any US arrival info, relationship to you, and copies of any documents you already have. Ask what records they might have and how to request copies.  
- Example opening: “Hello — I am researching my great‑aunt, [Full Name, née …], born [date] in [town]. She emigrated from Germany to the United States circa [year]. I’m seeking any records (DP, migration, naturalization, camp registrations). Attached are scans of the documents I have. Any guidance or records you could point me to would be much appreciated.”

7) If you want active help, I can:
- Draft emails/letters to archives (Arolsen, NARA, USCIS, USHMM).  
- Create search queries for Ancestry/FamilySearch (name variants, wildcard examples).  
- Suggest how to interpret a ship manifest or naturalization record you find.  
- Provide templates for asking relatives for stories or documents.

8) Privacy & timelines
- Some records require proof of death or next‑of‑kin status or have access restrictions. Response times vary (weeks to months). Arolsen and national archives often have request forms and may charge copying fees.

---

